@page "/car"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@inject EvInfo Vehicle
@inject ChargingLimits Limits
@inject ConfiguredVehicleFeed Feed

@*
    The car, configured from a browser (issue #214). The other side of the cable from /pv-system, on
    the same mechanism and with the same split: the form at the top edits what the NEXT start reads,
    and everything under it is what this process resolved at startup and is running with.

    Configuring the car used to be the last thing here that could only be done over ssh, and it is the
    thing a new owner does first — spread across four configuration sections that nothing said were
    about the same car, two of which together are a startup exception. A <select> makes that
    unrepresentable.

    What stays on /vehicle-portal, and why it did not move: this page is "which car, and which
    account"; that one is "does that account work" — a sign-in that needs an emailed one-time code, a
    MyŠkoda key pasted into a live store, and the field diagnostics that answer "why is battery blank
    on this model". A save here cannot prove any of that, so it links there instead.
*@

<SiteTitle Page="Car" />

<h1>Car</h1>

<p class="muted">
    Everything after the form is what the controller is running with, resolved from the configuration
    at startup. The form changes what the next start reads; a restart applies it.
</p>

<CarEditorForm />

<h2>Running</h2>

@if (!Vehicle.IsConfigured)
{
    <p class="muted" id="car-unconfigured">
        Nothing is described yet, so every charging limit is the installation's own and no target can be
        asked for as a state of charge. That is a supported installation — the charge still runs — and
        the form above is how to change it.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Name</dt>
        <dd>@Text(Vehicle.Name)</dd>

        <dt>Id</dt>
        <dd>@Text(Vehicle.Id)</dd>

        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>@Text(string.Join(" ", new[] { Vehicle.Make, Vehicle.Model }.Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part))))</dd>

        <dt>Usable battery</dt>
        <dd>@(Vehicle.CanTargetSoc ? $"{Vehicle.BatteryCapacityKWh.ToString("0.#", Invariant)} kWh" : Missing)</dd>

        <dt>Charge efficiency</dt>
        <dd>@Vehicle.ChargeEfficiency.ToString("P0", Invariant)</dd>
    </dl>

    @*
        Three columns rather than one, because the interesting number is the third: what the car says,
        what the installation says, and which of them the controller is actually working to.
    *@
    <table class="grid">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>The car</th><th>The installation</th><th>In effect</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Minimum current</td>
                <td>@Amps(Vehicle.MinChargingCurrentAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MinAmps == Vehicle.MinChargingCurrentAmps ? null : Limits.MinAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MinAmps)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Maximum current</td>
                <td>@Amps(Vehicle.MaxChargingCurrentAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MaxAmps == Vehicle.MaxChargingCurrentAmps ? null : Limits.MaxAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MaxAmps)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Phases</td>
                <td>@Count(Vehicle.Phases)</td>
                <td>@Count(Limits.Phases == Vehicle.Phases ? null : Limits.Phases)</td>
                <td>@Count(Limits.Phases)</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="table-note muted">
        The controller works to the narrower of the two. A car can only ever <em>lower</em> a limit —
        the installation's maximum is the site's supply and the wiring in the wall, not a preference —
        and anything the car leaves unstated is simply the installation's figure.
    </p>
}

<h2>Feed</h2>

@*
    Which feed this process actually started, as opposed to which one the configuration asks for. They
    differ in exactly one case worth showing: the Data Act portal beside a live feed, which #212 sets
    aside with a log line nobody reads twice.
*@
@if (Feed.Service is { } service)
{
    <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Reading the car</dt>
        <dd>@service.DisplayName <span class="muted">(@service.Manufacturer)</span></dd>

        <dt>For</dt>
        <dd>@Text(service.VehicleId)</dd>

        <dt>State</dt>
        <dd>@service.Health.State — @service.Health.Message</dd>
    </dl>
}
else
{
    <p class="muted" id="no-feed">
        No manufacturer feed is running. Charging is unaffected; what is missing is the car's own state
        of charge, so a target is asked for in kWh rather than as a percentage.
    </p>
}

@if (Feed.SetAside is { } setAside)
{
    <p class="table-note muted" id="feed-set-aside">@setAside</p>
}

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Vehicle.TelemetryTopic))
{
    <p class="table-note muted" id="own-topic">
        This car also has a topic of its own, <code>@Vehicle.TelemetryTopic</code>. It may run beside a
        manufacturer feed — the freshest reading wins.
    </p>
}

<p class="table-note muted">
    None of this says whether the feed is <em>signed in</em>.
    <a href="/vehicle-portal">Vehicle portal</a> is where an account is proved: sign in there, press
    <em>Ask the car</em>, and what comes back is what this configuration is worth.
</p>

@code {
    private const string Missing = "—";

    private static readonly System.Globalization.CultureInfo Invariant =
        System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture;

    private static string Text(string value) => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? Missing : value;

    private static string Amps(int? value) => value is { } amps ? $"{amps} A" : Missing;

    private static string Count(int? value) => value is { } number ? number.ToString(Invariant) : Missing;
}
